e pytest run, section (a)'s assert must have FAILED at line ~140-ish... but pytest reported 154. \n\nWait — maybe pytest DID report the first failure and the `-v` output tail I saw was only the last chunk. The line 154 assert was `assert rc.find(vw.Slider)` in section (b). If section (a)'s assert had failed first, pytest would report that line instead. So section (a) passed with value `{\"num_agents\": 10}`?? \n\nHmm, hold on. Let me look at the SECOND version of the file again... I need to check what I actually wrote. In the second version I wrote:\n\n```python\ndef test_creator_parameter_tracking():\n    \"\"\"ModelCreator initializes parameter tracking with/without initial params.\"\"\"\n    # (a) shared reactive starting empty (the SolaraViz path)\n    reactive_model = solara.reactive(KwargsModel(num_agents=7))\n    reactive_params = solara.reactive({})\n\n    @solara.component\n    def Harness():\n        ModelCreator(reactive_model, {}, model_parameters=reactive_params)\n```\n\nHmm wait, no! Actually I wrote `ModelCreator(reactive_model, {}, ...)`? Let me scroll up... The Write call had:\n\n```python\n    @solara.component\n    def Harness():\n        ModelCreator(reactive_model, {}, model_parameters=reactive_params)\n\n    with warnings.catch_warnings(record=True) as record:\n        warnings.simplefilter(\"always\")\n        solara.render(Harness(), handle_error=False)\n    assert not _conditional_warnings(record), _conditional_warnings(record)\n    # defaults from the constructor signature were tracked\n    assert reactive_params.value == {\"num_agents\": 10}, reactive_params.value\n```\n\nYes. And pytest reported failure at line 154 with `assert rc.find(vw.Slider)`. Since pytest stops at the first failed assert in a test, and the failure was at the slider line, the `{\"num_agents\": 10}` assert passed... \n\nUnless — pytest's `-v` tail output confused me: maybe the visible traceback was from `test_solara_viz_step_and_reset_buttons` and the two FAILED entri